been known for over a decade that  these two proteins bind to each other  and interact to regulate cell division  but there was little known about their  their functions during neural  development when i studied them  x-map 215 is is also called c-h-tog in  humans or c-cap 5.  now x-map 215 a lot was known about this  decades of work xmap was known as the  classic microtubule polymerase it would  bind tubulin dimers in the cytoplasm as  well as the end of microtubules the plus  end  and it would deposit those tubulin  dimers onto the plus end to promote  microtubule growth  thus it functioned as a catalytic  microtubule polymerase  it was also highly enriched at the  centrosome from where most from where  most microtubules nucleate and it's  thought that it's there so that exomap  213 could then load on to new  microtubules to promote their growth  what i'm going to tell you about today  though  is a new function for x-map-215  where it is more than simply microtubule  polymerase  now this work was spearheaded by a  postdoc in my lab paula slater as well  as a graduate student garrett camarata  and they had the help of two undergrads  annika and ali  when paula joined my lab she wanted to  explore uh an  unexpected finding uh for xmap 215  knocks down growth cones so i found that  when we knocked down x-map 215 this  catalytic microtubule polymerase but  only knocked it down partially like 50  we didn't get rid of it completely just  partially  we saw that the growth cones were larger  now this made little sense so in this in  this image you can see the green art is  actin  which forms like the size of the growth  cone and red are the microtubules  and when pollen measured  the these growth cones she found that  when x-map 215 was partially knocked  down the growth cones were larger and  the filopodia were longer  and she could rescue this by adding back  full length x-map 215 suggesting that  this defect was specific to x-map 215  knockdown  this made little sense why knocking down  a microtubule polymerase would lead to  larger growth cones longer filopodia  filled with actin  we wanted to know well could x-map-215  be somehow affecting f actin  now in a movie that i had taken you  could see the still image where xmap 215  this is a growth cone  you could see that it forms these puncta  at the ends of microtubules but also you  could see it along the microtubule  lattice the side of the microtubule  this led us to wonder well  in order for microtubules to grow along  these f-actin bundles  the so the f actin is also undergoing a  process called retrograde flow you can  see this in the bottom left cartoon  where actin is flowing backwards towards  the center of the growth tone but the  microtubules are moving forward but if  the microtubules are going to be  moving along the f actin using them as  guides there has to be transient  frequent coupling and uncoupling  regulation of the interaction between  microtubules and effects and we wondered  could xmf215 be playing a role in this  interaction  so apollo looked  at the alignment of the microtubules to  these f-actin bundles  and she found that with the x-map 215  knock-down condition  there was a reduction in the percentage  of microtubules that were aligned to f  actin again this could be rescued by  adding back x and up to 15. and if she  overexpressed x map 215 there was an  increase  in the number of microtubules that were  aligned to the f-actin and so this  suggested that yes indeed x-map-215 can  affect microtubule alignment with  f-actin in growth counts  so she wanted to know well what what  part of x-map 215 was facilitating this  interaction so this is a cartoon of  x-men it's a big protein it's 215  kilodaltons there's a number of domains  there are five tog domains tog stands  for tumor over express gene and there's  a c term domain  and so she made a series of of deletion  constructs to see which domains could  rescue  the xmap 215 knockdown microtubule  f-actin alignment defect and here's a  busy graph what you can see from this is  i'm going to focus your attention on  this is what it looks like when the full  length x215 is expressed there's a full  rescue  of the  alignment defect but when she looked at  all these various constructs none of  them could rescue like the full length  except for this tog one to five so tog  one to four was not sufficient  and so the difference between top one to  four and tug one to five is not only the  fifth top domain but also this  microtubule lattice binding region  and that led us to wonder perhaps  something in this region is allowing the  microtubules  to to interact with with effect and  through binding to x-map 215 on this  lattice  but we wanted to know this was the very  first time maximum 215 had been  associated with f actin  so can it actually bind f-actin in vitro  is this even possible  so to to test this  garrett my phd student used an f-actin  sedimentation assay where he combined  polymerized f-actin along with purified  x-mep 215 protein and then combine them  together spun them down anything that  bound to the pellet  was  binding to f-actin and anything the  supernatant  was not binding to fact and what he  found was that indeed  x-map 215 could directly bind to f-actin  and vitro you can see as the levels of  f-actin go up there's increasing levels  of x-map 215 in the pellet okay and this  shows very strong binding to f actin  and so this is the full length and so of  course the next question is what domains  of xmph215 facilitate this interaction  with f actin  and so again we did deletion construct  analysis the c term domain was not  sufficient to bind to f action you could  see there's almost no x map 215 c term  in the pellet in this in this image  the tog one to four was not sufficient  for strong binding to the pel to the f  actin very littles in the pellet but the  tug one to five the same construct that  could rescue the microtubule effect and  alignment 