hen we partially knock down tach3  there's a reduction in the velocity of  microtubule growth and when we  overexpress tach3  there is a slight increase in the  velocity of microtubule growth the  lifetime of the microtubules were not  affected so the catastrophe frequency  wasn't affected just the polymerization  rate  now consistently with this effect on  microtubules brazil found that when she  reduced tachy levels there was a  reduction in the length of the axons  that grew out when there was an increase  in temporary levels there was an  increase in the axon outgrowth and this  was all expected for a microtubule  polymerase promoter  what was fascinating though  is when uh bergeron looked at time-lapse  imaging  she saw that overexpressing tac-3  led to a mild reduction in accent  outgrowth velocity this made no sense  if we are over expressing a microtubule  polymerase and the axons end up being  longer why would they grow out more  slowly how could that be  when she looked at the data more closely  she noticed that in controlled  situations  axons growing on laminin coated  coverslips in vitro  they undergo spontaneous retraction at a  certain frequency and when taxi was  overexpressed  the axons reduced their spontaneous  retraction rates  thus these axons even though they were  growing more slowly they grew more  persistently over time and that is how  they end up having longer axons  now this was interesting to us and we we  wanted to know more about  whether taxi could be mediating accident  outgrowth in response to guidance cues  so  what brazil did is she applied  slit 2 which is normally a repulsive  factor and she found that  normally when you add slit 2 to  the media of these axons there's a  collapse of the rocks and you can't see  it the movie isn't playing but if you if  you were watching the movie you'd see  the axon would be collapsing  what she found is that when taxi was  overexpressed  there was a reduction in the growth cone  collapse phenotype thus this data  demonstrated to us that tax free was  somehow mediating the growth cone  response to guidance few such as slip  we wondered well we wanted to know more  about the signaling pathway involved and  so the first candidate we looked at was  ableton tyrosine kinase it's known that  able mediate split signaling we wanted  to know could ableton tyrosine kinase be  directly indirectly or directly  phosphorylating tact three and so to do  this work we joined forces with a  collaborator of mine brian ballas at the  university of vermont  he's a mass back and kinase expert what  we found is that when we express tack  ray gfp as well as ableton tyrosine  kinase in heck 293 cells there was this  really nice phosphotyrosine signal  we sent that off from mass back there  were a number of candidates the number  of tyrosines that were phosphorylated  both in the attack domain as well as a  highly conserved regulatory domain  and so we wanted to know if we knocked  those out you know could it affect the  function of path3  when she knocked it out first she did a  western and surprisingly saw that the  phospho-tyrosine signal was not changed  at all so then she knocked out the other  uh possible tyrosines it could be  phosphorylated did a western again after  expressing them and again there was  still a phosphotyrosine signal which  again was surprising so then she decided  well there's other tyrosines in tech  three and maybe they're being  phosphorylated you know when these when  some of the tyrosines are missing so  then she decided to focus on just attack  domain which is sufficient to drive  polymerization and there are six  tyrosines there so she made phosphonols  all fixed again she did the expression  analysis and the uh western and she saw  surprisingly that there was still a  fossil tyrosine signal despite the fact  that there were no tyrosines left intact  but then she realized that we were  expressing gfp attack not just tac gfp  also has tyrosines so when she expressed  just gfp alone  she found that when able was  co-expressed gfp could be phosphorylated  and this is something that's really  important for me to share with you  because i didn't realize that gfp could  be phosphorylated and it isn't in the  literature anywhere so this is something  that people should keep in mind if  they're doing these sorts of experiments  but we still have this hack phosphor  knowledge and she wanted to know does  the fossil null change tax function  and here what she did was she expressed  tac uh both the wild type tech as well  as tax phosphonol and she found that  under uh control conditions you know  wild type tack most of it bound to the  microtubule but the attack phosphonol  most of it found on most of it did not  bind the microtubule and instead was  stuck in the cytoplasm  okay so to summarize what i shared for  you about new tax refunction  first  bonding to microtubules to promote their  polymerization  too much tax rate leads to microtubule  growth despite the repulsive cues and  reduced acid retraction and less leading  to longer axons and that attack  phosphonol cannot bind as well to  microtubules so kinase signaling does  indeed affect tech's ability to bind to  microtubules and regulate external  guidance  and future studies in my lab are  continuing to dissect the mechanisms by  which tac-3 is regulated during growth  guidance  now in the last uh you know a few  minutes of my talk i want to turn to  another area of research in my lab uh  looking at wolf horse run syndrome  now this relates to neurocrystals so i'm  going to give you a brief introduction  to them  neurocrest cells are a multi-potent  migratory embryonic cell type that they  laminate from the neural tube during  development and they migrate throughout  the embryo and become craniofacial  structures heart  skeletal muscle peripheral neurons  many different cell types and this work  was spearheaded by a former phd in my  lab beth pierce  now we became interested in this  disorder will fortune syndrome  when  we realized that tax rate which we were  studying in the in the context of axon  guidance for some time we learned the  tactic was also deleted in this human  disorder called wolferson syndrome or  whf there's a number of genes missing in  this disorder it's a hemisphygus  mutation  and the embryonic function of most of  these genes was unknown  the defects these children have are  craniofacial heart skeletal cognitive  function defects a number of these  defects could involve a defect in neural  crest cell migration so we wondered if  it could be a neurocrystal migration  disorder so we decided to test that by  looking at the functions of these  different genes expression and the  effect on knockdown  so first we found that most of these  associated genes are expressed at least  in part in neural crest cells and so  here on the left you can see a  neurocrystal marker and it forms uh it  stains these pharyngeal arches on the  side with you can see it's pointing to  with the arrowheads  and we're looking at the side views of  these heads of these um embryon xenopus  embryos and the big blob that you can  see are the eyes and these pharyngeal  arches which form various craniofacial  structures on the side and most of these  genes are at least partially expressed  in these pharyngeal arches suggesting  they are expressed in the right place to  be playing a role in neural crest cells  when we knocked each of them down  individually we found that they also  impacted not an impact of craniofacial  morphology facial width and midface area  are here but they affected other  parameters as well  we also found that they could affect the  migration of the pharyngeal arches so  when we only knock down  one side of the embryo with either whs1  or tach3 you can see that the pharyngeal  arches are reduced in size and we  wondered could it be because the  pharyngeal arches can't the cells can't  migrate as far so to test this we  dissected out the neural crest cells and  plated them and then measured motility  parameters  we found that indeed knocking down whf  c1 reduces neurocrystal motility in  vitro as well as knocking down tac-3  we did a number of other uh assays which  i don't have time to talk about today um  but what we found is that numerous genes  in this region contribute to various  aspects of craniofacial patterning so  all of them were expressed in the neural  crest cells  when we knock all of them down we see  various defects in facial morphology  we see defects in cartilage morphology  in when we knock down two of them  when we see defects in infrared arch  length and neural crest cell migration  when we knock down two of them  and then we also see defects in four  brain size so they they contribute to  different aspects of craniofacial  patterning  and continued work in my lab is now  investigating well how does tac-3  specifically affect neurocrystal  motility but that's an answer for the  future  so to summarize what i presented  to you today  first i i hope that i've been able to  convey that xenopus lavis is a fantastic  embryonic cell model a model system to  for study cell migration various various  contexts  eczema 215 not only is the microtubule  polymerase but it also can couple or  align microtubule growth microgrowth  microtubules with f-actin tac 3 is a  plus tip that promotes  microtubularization during axon  outgrowth and guidance  i've also  shown you works showing that tax iii  likely contributes to the whs phenotype  along with the other candidate genes  and  the current focus of research in my lab  moving forward is studying how taxi  specifically promotional crystal  migration and also i didn't  tell you about two projects looking at  how collective cell movements are  regulated as well as we're now  transitioning to using benefits as a  model system to study individual  melanoma metastasis  with that i want to acknowledge those  who contribute to